WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:02.700
Yes.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.110
I’m a biochemist.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:18.009
I, I did my thesis work working on bacteria,
and then I switched to, to plants.

00:00:18.009 --> 00:00:20.050
Um, bio- the biochemistry of plants.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:25.029
And I’ve worked on topics such as: how do
plants make lipids and how do plants make

00:00:25.029 --> 00:00:26.029
cellulose.

00:00:26.029 --> 00:00:31.179
So, the kinds of materials that are actually
used to make biofuels, my, I spent my career

00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:35.989
working on the basic mechanisms of how those
things are made by plants.

00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:42.590
Um, I got interested in the, in the field,
I switched to plants specifically in the 70s

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:47.690
when I was a student because I was very concerned
about the sustainability of our modern societies.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:53.359
And, that was the era in which people like
Paul Ehrlich were talking about, um, the population

00:00:53.359 --> 00:00:54.359
bomb.

00:00:54.359 --> 00:01:01.110
And, so my wife and I, my wife is also a professor
at Berkeley, we decided that we needed to

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:10.250
start, um, trying to apply science to making
at least part of our material and food needs

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:11.250
more sustainable.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:16.480
And so we’ve spent our time, uh, most of
our scientific careers working on trying to

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:22.240
improve our ability to manipulate plants,
uh, for, for both agriculture and fuel uses.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:29.310
And I’ve published about 230 papers in the
field.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:33.120
Yes.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:39.910
The Energy Biosciences Institute at Berkeley
was founded by, uh, in response to a, um,

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:45.810
a solicitation by the big energy company BP
for an institute that would focus on trying

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:51.700
to understand how energy could be, or sorry,
how, how modern biology could be applied to

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:52.700
the energy sector.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.810
And it was a very, kind of bo- bold idea.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:59.580
They were looking around for technical innovation,
and to make a long story short, when I was

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:06.180
still a professor at Stanford, I, I got involved
with a person named Steve Chu at, uh, UC Berkeley,

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:10.679
and Steve went on to become the Secretary
of Energy of the United States.

00:02:10.679 --> 00:02:17.430
Um, and we, um, had decided that Berkeley
had a lot of capability in renewable energy,

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:22.610
and that we should try to get this, um, this
new center that BP was offering to fund with

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:24.459
500 million dollars.

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:30.069
So, we, we wrote a proposal, it was successful,
and then Berkeley recruited me away from Stanford

00:02:30.069 --> 00:02:32.079
to go and lead that center.

00:02:32.079 --> 00:02:35.519
And now the center has about, um, all together
about 500 people.

00:02:35.519 --> 00:02:42.819
It was 127 professors and, um, uh, about,
um, 380 graduate students and post docs.

00:02:42.819 --> 00:02:47.109
And we have a, it’s not just at Berkeley,
it’s at Berkeley and, uh, the University

00:02:47.109 --> 00:02:52.170
of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign because Berkeley’s
not a very big agricultural school, and we

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:57.430
wanted to have a, a footprint out in the agricultural
region of the country.

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:01.280
And at that institute, we’re taking a holistic
view of bioenergy.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:06.640
So we, we do economics, and environmental
science, and ecology, and mechanical engineering,

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:13.400
chemical engineering, chemistry, biochemistry,
microbiology, and, uh, law and policy, and

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:14.400
agronomy.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:20.510
All wrapped up into one coherent whole, to
try and understand how to advance, uh, well,

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:23.670
how to implement advanced, uh, biofuels.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:30.109
Uh, we don’t work at all on, um, sugar cane
or corn ethanol or biodiesel.

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:33.879
Partially, mostly because those are already
relatively mature.

00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:38.040
So we’re really focused on next generation
fuels.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:50.049
Well there’s, I think, I, what I try and
convey in my talk is that I, I think in the,

00:03:50.049 --> 00:03:54.590
in most aspects of bioenergy there’s a lot
of opportunity for innovation.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:59.799
There’s still, there’s still many aspects
of the process where we don’t even know

00:03:59.799 --> 00:04:04.189
what the optimal route forward is, or too,
what’s, what’s ultimately going to be,

00:04:04.189 --> 00:04:06.209
I assume, a mature technology.

00:04:06.209 --> 00:04:12.209
So, um, I think there’s lots of interesting
opportunities to get involved with testing

00:04:12.209 --> 00:04:17.290
new ideas and trying to move them towards
commercialization.

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:23.230
My experience has been there’s quite a bit
of venture capital still available for different

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:25.940
components, uh, of the, of the sector.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:30.100
I, myself, um, have started, uh, three companies.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:33.070
Two of which were in the bioenergy space.

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:36.950
Um, and are, two, are both thriving.

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:42.530
So I, um, I, I think that’s a really exciting
opportunity.

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:47.340
And it’s something that requires innovation
and, you know, new i- new thinking.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:53.620
And so, um, I think that’s something that
small, small, ah, companies are really well

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:54.930
qualified to do.

00:04:54.930 --> 00:05:01.031
And then eventually I expect, um, the large
energy companies will, ul, ultimately come

00:05:01.031 --> 00:05:04.660
to dominate the sort of the, the very last
step of the process.

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:09.890
That is, you know, the large scale production,
but that they will be large consumers of,

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:19.760
of interesting technology.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:25.780
I got into the field really because, um, I
was very concerned about climate change.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:31.470
So, when I was a professor at Stanford, actually,
Paul Ehrlich, the father of conservation biology,

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:34.620
was one of my colleagues in the department
of biological sciences.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:38.080
And, ah, Stanford also is a very strong ecology
school.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:44.960
And my colleagues in ecology in particular,
really convinced me that the consequences

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:52.930
of climate change were, were serious and something
that we really should try to, to bring every

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:56.370
possible, um, technology to bear on.

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:58.810
So, so, I started.

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:05.180
That’s how I became involved with Steve
Chu, um, and I was, ah, for some years an

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:10.080
advisor to the department of energy, to the
director of the department of energy as well.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.990
And I got, in that process, I got somewhat
educated about sort of, what the challenges

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:20.300
were, and s- gradually turned my own research
towards, towards trying to adjust that challenge.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:26.300
And I, I believe now that, actually, you know,
it is very challenging, but long term, I do

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:32.560
think that we can introduce a basket of technologies
that will allow us to, to evolve towards a

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:38.650
low carbon, um, energy system that will still
allow us to maintain, uh, a good standard

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:42.290
of living around the world, and, um.

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:47.940
So, I think it’s, I, actually, think it’s
really the great problem of this generation

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:54.650
is, is how we’re going to address the energy
problem, you know, the energy and climate

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:56.080
problem, shall we say.

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:58.400
And, um, I see many ways to do it.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:06:59.430
Not just in bioenergy.

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:05.500
At Berkeley, we have an integrated energy
group we call the Berkeley, uh, energy and

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:11.700
climate initiative that, that, in which we’ve
now got, I, I would estimate fifteen hundred

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:14.500
to two thousand scientists working across
the whole space.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:17.920
So, some of us are working on biofuels.

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:20.050
Many are working on energy efficiency.

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:25.600
Many are working on solar and smart grid and,
um, ge- and, geological sequestration.

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:31.830
And in general I think that that’s the,
the big opportunity now, is to, is to look

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:36.150
at everything we can possibly do, and, uh,
bring as much creativity as we can to the

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:37.150
sector.

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:42.060
And, and I think, you know, within the next
50 years, we’ll be able to gradually change

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:44.950
the society to, to a low carbon society.

00:07:44.950 --> 00:07:49.970
And I think it’s very important to understand
it is a, you know, a fifty year or beyond

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:50.970
kind of initiative.

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:56.170
That we can’t expect, uh, short term, um,
you know, changes.

00:07:56.170 --> 00:08:01.220
And in fact, I think one of our biggest challenges
is that the popular press, which is ver- you

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:05.840
know, has, plays an important role in understanding
the problem, uh, has a very short kind of,

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:10.671
uh, um, eh, interest, um, they, or they assume
people have a short interest.

00:08:10.671 --> 00:08:13.880
And so, they’re always looking for, you
know, the short term solution.

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:17.580
But actually, we’re engaged in something
that requires a long term commitment.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:21.860
And so, I hope, my, one of my big hopes is
that the popular press will also get involved

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:27.490
in, sort of, in keeping people’s, uh, interest
and enthusiasm up as we make incremental change

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:29.680
towards a long term solution.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:38.329
Yes, ah, well, I, I think, for me it would
be biofuels.

00:08:38.329 --> 00:08:44.999
Because right now, it’s the one that we
do know how to produce and it, it, I believe

00:08:44.999 --> 00:08:50.710
it can be done sustainably, and, and it actually
can be done locally and, um, it’s how we

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:52.060
used to produce most of our energy.

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:56.670
You know, it wasn’t that long ago that,
uh, that actually we got pretty much all our

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:57.699
energy from wood.

00:08:57.699 --> 00:09:03.610
And, uh, and in fact, uh, there’s a funny
saying that, um, in the United States, let’s

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:08.200
see, at the turn of, uh, of the, uh, at the
end of the 1900s, no at the en- the end of

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:14.269
the 1800s, so in 1900, a third of the land
in the United States roughly was used to make

00:09:14.269 --> 00:09:15.389
transportation fuel.

00:09:15.389 --> 00:09:19.899
And, of course, in that day, it was growing
oats for the horses that provided pretty much

00:09:19.899 --> 00:09:21.769
all transportation.

00:09:21.769 --> 00:09:27.130
And so, so I actually see the bioenergy sort
of initiative as leading us back towards,

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:30.690
uh, a sustainable, sort of a sustainable thing.

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:33.730
And, um, so we, we knew how to do it, and
we’re gonna relearn.

00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:37.500
Of course now our energy demand has increased
very strongly, so we need to be a lot better

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:38.500
at it than we were in 1900.

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:39.500
Well, we’ve sort of seen that occasionally,
right?

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:40.500
There’ve been some pretty big, um, blackouts
in which, um, you know, the power was down

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:41.500
for substantial periods of time.

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:42.500
In fact, when I was a professor at Stanford,
the power went down here for four days, um,

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:43.500
uh, due to some sort of, due to a storm that
blew through and put down a lot of power lines.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:44.500
And so, uh, I remember it as being a very
very frustrating period of time, because I

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:45.500
couldn’t do my email.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:46.500
[Laughs] So, uh, yeah.

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:47.500
So I think, um, obviously, it’s unpleasant
to, to live without, without, uh, power.

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:48.500
And hopefully we won’t have too many future
instances of it.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:49.500
Now, I’m not all sure what else to say about
that.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:49.501
[laughs]

